Title:
Extension Hollowing: Abusing Chrome’s Extension Trust Model

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post describes “Extension Hollowing,” a technique to covertly convert an already-installed legitimate Chrome Web Store extension into a “custom/unpacked” extension so an attacker can modify its code without Chrome’s normal integrity protections. It builds on the author’s Silent_Chrome work, detailing how recent Chrome changes (e.g., new `developer_mode_encrypted_hash` handling and signed-in user `account_values` paths) impacted silent enabling of Developer Mode and how those checks can be bypassed by manipulating Chrome preference files. The core abuse is that extension identity (ID) can be spoofed via the `key` in `manifest.json`, and Chrome will prefer a custom-loaded version over the Web Store version while keeping the same extension ID and on-disk location artifacts. The post also covers forcing immediate load of modified code by deleting Service Worker `ScriptCache` and `Database` directories, and notes edge cases where incorrect Secure Preferences fields trigger user-visible extension errors. It demonstrates bypassing enterprise extension allowlisting and discusses a macOS-specific issue where hollowing can “boot” force-installed extensions from the session. Defensive guidance focuses on policy hardening (disabling Developer Mode) and monitoring for non-Chrome processes modifying Chrome preference/extension files.

Technical Focus:
- Chrome Secure Preferences / Preferences / Local State tampering
- Developer Mode enablement and MAC/HMAC protection fields (`protection.macs.*`)
- Extension ID spoofing via `manifest.json` `key` and custom vs Web Store precedence
- Service Worker cache invalidation (ScriptCache/Database) to force code reload
- Enterprise extension controls (allowlisting, `ExtensionInstallForcelist`, `ExtensionDeveloperModeSettings`)
- Cross-platform considerations (macOS Keychain, Windows app-bound encryption, Linux behavior)

Use Cases:
- Covert persistence and execution via a trusted, already-installed Chrome extension
- Bypassing enterprise extension allowlists by “hollowing” an allowed extension
- Post-compromise credential/session theft or web traffic manipulation via injected content scripts/service worker code
- Red team tradecraft for blending (retain extension ID/UI behavior while adding malicious logic)
- Blue team detection engineering for Chrome profile/extension integrity monitoring

Keywords:
Chrome, Chrome Extensions, Extension Hollowing, Silent_Chrome, Secure Preferences, Preferences, Local State, developer_mode, developer_mode_encrypted_hash, protection.macs, HMAC, manifest.json, extension ID, manifest key, unpacked extension, Web Store integrity, enterprise policy, ExtensionInstallForcelist, ExtensionDeveloperModeSettings, Service Worker, ScriptCache, macOS Keychain, Windows app-bound encryption, Mythic, Hades agent, content scripts, background service worker, file integrity monitoring